From 71d1fd5024cfae49643b3365430a56850990c794 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 10:01:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(kanban board): refresh after a commit re-parses only
 changed tasks

The board's retained rows were rebuilt from scratch on every commit to
kanban.db (the hub's board commits ~40x/min, half of them heartbeats), so
each 20 s Desktop refresh paid ~0.6-0.9 s CPU re-parsing every task row and
re-reading every event for diagnostics, plus parsing ~4.2k done rows that
the done-column cap drops.

- board_rows.materialized takes an ``update(prev)`` used instead of
  ``build`` when older rows exist for the key; the board passes the prior
  rows to board_read.materialize.
- materialize still reads every task row (a committed write is never
  missed) but reuses the parsed Task/asdict of rows whose values (and
  column names) are unchanged, and re-reads events only for tasks whose
  row, runs or event (count, max id) moved; marks are read before the
  events they vouch for.
- Done cards past the cap are ranked on a narrow (rowid, id, completed_at)
  read and never fetched whole (kanban_db.list_task_rows / list_tasks
  extra_where + parse).

Hub kanban.db (4.4k tasks, 246 on the board), CPU per refresh after a
commit: 580-960 ms -> 200-320 ms; warm cold read 590-730 -> 320-440 ms.
